# Compilation protocol

Use the installed, authenticated `codex` CLI to compile natural-language source. Run Codex from the project root with read-only workspace access and a structured JSON output schema. Pass an absolute temporary response path with `-o` and an absolute schema path with `--output-schema`; those flags are part of the existing CLI and let offline compiler fixtures exercise the same protocol. The Rust runner alone writes generated and inferred files. Surface Codex failures and diagnostic output to the user. Keep the normal compiler transcript and the submitted spec text out of the terminal on success. Report `Compiling all N spec(s) with Codex` immediately for a full build, or `Compiling N changed spec(s) with Codex; M generated file(s) affected` for a scoped build. Follow with periodic `Still compiling with Codex (Ns elapsed)` updates while it works.

## Full compilation input

Give the compiler every human spec's original Markdown and hashes of its directly linked local references, plus the previous inferred assumptions and semantic record when present. Tell it to inspect local runtime availability before choosing an interpreter or GUI toolkit. It may infer missing product semantics, but each assumption must be explicit in the response. It should generate a minimal runnable program and meaningful checks, with no features invented beyond the specs and surfaced assumptions.

The compiler instructions for full and scoped builds explicitly say `.nat files are Markdown` and direct the compiler to `view referenced images` with read-only tools. In a scoped build, include the changed sources and affected old output content, and omit unrelated source and output content.

## Full compilation output

Use a JSON object with `summary`, `requirements`, `assumptions`, `files`, `run`, and `checks`:

- Each requirement has `id`, `source`, and `statement`.
- Each assumption has `id`, `source`, `statement`, and `reason`.
- Each file has `path`, full `content`, and `sources` listing all `.nat` files it directly or indirectly depends on. If unsure, list all source paths.
- `run` is an argv array; `checks` is an array of argv arrays. These commands run from `generated/` without a shell. An empty `run` means the project is not runnable.

Require nonempty valid IDs and mapped requirements, existing source paths, unique IDs and output paths, and safe relative generated paths. Reject paths that escape `generated/`, use symlinks, collide with a parent file, or use reserved `ir.json` or `.nat-manifest.json`. Reject empty argv elements. JSON output schemas constrain `source` and `sources` to the exact source paths supplied; still validate saved `--proposal` results in Rust.
When a semantic record has an invalid ID or source, name its kind and ID in the diagnostic; for example, `invalid assumption A-01 or source`.

For compatibility with older full-build responses, accept a file without `sources` from `--proposal JSON` or the installed compiler and attribute it conservatively to all current `.nat` sources. The schema for new compiler output still requires `sources`. Scoped responses must retain explicit dependencies.

Before applying a result, verify that tracked generated and inferred files have not been edited or deleted. Reject overwriting an untracked file. Validate the whole response before writing any file. Render `generated/ir.json` with the summary, requirements, and assumptions; render `inferred/assumptions.inat` with reviewable assumption text and source/reason. Each inferred item includes a line `Source: spec/<exact source path>` and retains the full reason text. Record hashes, commands, dependency lists, and summary in `generated/.nat-manifest.json`. Remove outputs no longer in a successful full result. Avoid rewriting files whose bytes have not changed.

## Verified reuse without retranscription

Full semantic scope does not require printing every unchanged byte. For a rebuild,
freeze a verified snapshot of the previous tracked outputs before invoking Codex.
Include a compact catalog of their relative paths, content hashes and dependencies
in the full-build input and explain that the compiler may read them locally.
The new full response keeps the complete files inventory, but each file's content
may be a string (new complete bytes) or null (reuse the exact supplied prior file
at this path). Null is an interpreter-resolved reference, not an empty file and
not permission to use arbitrary filesystem content. It is legal only for an
advertised, previously tracked output with bytes matching the frozen snapshot.
The model supplies current sources for both forms; dependencies remain conservative.
Do not narrow semantic scope or dependency sets merely to save output tokens.

Resolve null entries entirely in the runner using the frozen catalog and verified
bytes. Validate the assembled complete result under all ordinary path, identity,
source and command rules before applying it. Recheck source and prior artifact
hashes immediately before writes. Refuse stale, missing, symlinked, untracked or
unadvertised reuse targets without changing generated files, inferred source or
the manifest. Never substitute empty content or silently drop an invalid entry.
Omitted prior outputs retain ordinary full-build deletion semantics. A fresh
build cannot reuse files. Reused files keep their exact bytes and are not rewritten.

Imported legacy full --proposal responses with string contents remain supported.
For --proposal null entries, freeze and verify the current tracked build as the
explicit reuse basis at invocation; report this basis in diagnostics. They do not
refer to an unknown earlier model session. Scoped compilation retains its existing
response protocol and authority rules; this extension is for full responses only.

## Explicit unsuccessful compilation

New full compiler responses require status complete or incomplete and a diagnostic
string in addition to the existing fields. An incomplete response must be rejected
before applying any artifact regardless of whether files is empty or contains a
diagnostic placeholder. Preserve the response and report its diagnostic, leaving
all prior generated/inferred bytes and manifests unchanged. A complete response
still requires all existing structural checks; the flag is not proof of behavioral
correctness. Legacy imported full proposals and older fixture responses without
status are treated as legacy complete responses and validated as before.

Retain rejected model output in a diagnostic location outside generated/ and
inferred/, reporting its path. Do not invoke the compiler again automatically for
an explicit incomplete full response. Never replace the installed compiler during
self-regeneration. Whole-result structural validation precedes publication;
behavioral tests remain separately reported rather than assumed from status.

## Acceptance for verified reuse

Offline fixtures must exercise the actual CLI/schema path, not just helper functions:
- A full rebuild changes one file and reuses another by null content, preserving
  reused bytes and metadata, then passes check and executes the declared checks.
- A full response consisting entirely of valid reused files is legal when semantic
  records/source hashes change but implementation bytes do not.
- A fresh build, missing/untracked path, wrong-case path, duplicate path, symlink,
  edited prior bytes, changed source during compilation, and removed prior artifact
  all refuse reuse without changing existing output/inferred/manifest snapshots.
- A model returns incomplete with one diagnostic file and plausible commands:
  preserve its response and the entire prior build, with no successful-build claim.
- Legacy full proposals and existing scoped fixtures continue to work unchanged.
Measure response bytes saved using a real previous candidate, while distinguishing
that offline encoding comparison from live token usage or wall-clock savings.
